"36. Are there circumstances under which Reg E-Delivery should permit a covered entity to use an electronic address for a covered recipient that the covered entity receives from an affiliate or from a third party, rather than directly from the covered recipient? If so, what are those circumstances and how would permitting such use be consistent with the goal of ensuring that covered recipients who provide an electronic address are aware of how the electronic address will be used and what types of covered information will be electronically delivered to that electronic address? For example, are there circumstances under which an underwriter of a securities offering should be permitted to use an electronic address provided to the issuer in the offering, or vice versa? Or, if a covered recipient is onboarded though one covered entity but will receive products or services from another, affiliated covered entity that is part of the same enterprise, should the latter covered entity be permitted to use the electronic address that the covered recipient provided to the former covered entity? Or if a covered recipient is onboarded through one Conformed to Federal Register version covered entity that contracts with another, unaffiliated covered entity to provide services for the covered recipient (e.g., an introducing broker-dealer contracting with a clearing broker-dealer, or a separately managed account program sponsor contracting with an investment adviser managing accounts in the program), should the latter covered entity be permitted to use the electronic address that the covered recipient provided to the former covered entity?"

Yes - There are circumstances where covered entitys should be able to use confirmed emails through other means beyond the investor themselves - but crucially, it must be verifiable through association with other known identifying information. For example, if both the covered entity and the affiliate have an SSN, name, and address but only one has an email then that email could be provided as an alternative contact. There is no use in expanding information access in name of cost savings and efficiency if inaccuracy is introduced, to say nothing of the negative impact that would have on investors. This adjustment would be best considered as a proactive measure to ensure decentralization and scalability for operators within the transfer agent space, as while the robust legacy methods of contact and confirmation have worked well, there has been massive technological development within the space and commisurate with pendiong transfer agent regulation adjustments on the horizon per S7-2026-30 there should be changes made to prioritize easier compliance for TAs and to increase communication access for investors. 

While on the whole we agree with the concept of utilizing specifically email, there are some concerns if a third party would be allowed to provide a otherwise nonverified email address or similar electronic contact to a transfer agent to satisfy recordkeeping regulations and with expectation that the transfer agent would then use that provided contact in order to manage communications with the directly registered owner. However, some guardrails are absolutely necessary. While it would be convenient to jump straight to accepting these types of contacts through a third party for ease of transition and transfer, any removal of specific physical verification of the owner involved could introduce more distance between the managing transfer agent and the owner or limit the ability of the owner to make informed and current decisions on their directly held investments. 

Concerning the shape of these guardrails: There must be an opt-out notice as part of the transition to this policy, and the transition to this policy should be on a longer time scale for transfer agents. Individual investors should be prompted with the opportunity to preserve traditional paper contact and, if they see the value in moving to e-delivery, information should be traditionally validated. There are ways to create scalable confirmation outreach. One example could be having entities send out a paper mail with a link/QR code with an electronic form which would verify information about the recipient before providing details about the transition to the new information policies, giving the recipient the opportunity to provide such information directly and select preferences, and a timeline for the transition. Similar popups can be implemented on apps and on web clients. Considering the sizable potential savings available by transitioning to predominately e-delivery, it is worth the initial investment for covered entities to go through this process. A generous window of time can be provided so that there is time for design and implementation of these tools. As long as investors have an opportunity to review the information and to opt out, and that covered entities reach out to those investors for that clarity, then the industry can move forward into a more technologically friendly status quo that better reflects the modern day.

In securities-offering scenarios where participating entities in information exchanges are mostly dealing with beneficial owners and account holders rather than direct investors the process can be simpler. Have entities adjust their terms of service policies and contracts to reflect that information may be shared with other relevant entities. Persons should still be offered an opt out, and they must be clearly informed what kind of information qualifies to be shared and with which entities that information may be shared with. These privacy policy disclosures are common and they are adequate to provide complete context to users of a service. 
